{#-
  Condensed Regression Detector report for the Datadog Agent PR comment.

  Rendered client-side by the SMP CLI from `outputs/report.json`:

    smp report render \
      --template-file test/regression/report_template.md.j2 \
      --report-json outputs/report.json \
      --target-config-dir test/regression \
      --output-file outputs/report.md

  Design rule: a clean run must be near-silent. Only sections that need action
  are expanded; everything else is collapsed behind <details> but still carries
  the full table, so no information from the built-in `report.md` is lost.

  Because the Agent's enabled suite is entirely `quality_gate_*` experiments,
  the gate verdict leads and optimization goals follow.

  Notes on the render context (see SMP's docs/guides/reports.md):
  - Bounds figures describe the COMPARISON variant only. `report.json` also
    carries baseline replicates, but a bounds check asserts on the candidate
    code, so this template needs no variant filtering of its own.
  - `is_required` is SMP's own `experiment.startswith("quality_gate_")` test, so
    the gate list here matches what Agent CI gates on.
  - The pass/fail shown here is a DISPLAY verdict. The job's exit code comes
    from `smp job result --signal bounds-check`, which additionally ignores
    experiments configured `erratic: true`. Every Agent quality gate is
    `erratic: false`, so the two agree; if a gate is ever marked erratic, this
    report would show a failure that does not fail CI.
-#}
{#- Terse goal label, and which sign of Δ mean % is the improving direction.
    Keys are SMP's own goal display strings (payloads OptimizationGoalV9). -#}
{%- set GOALS = {
      "% cpu utilization":  ["cpu", "down"],
      "memory utilization": ["memory", "down"],
      "ingress throughput": ["throughput", "up"],
      "egress throughput":  ["egress throughput", "up"],
    } -%}
{%- set REPO = "https://github.com/DataDog/datadog-agent" -%}
{#- Per-experiment links: the configured logs link first, then each
    `report_links` entry from the experiment's own experiment.yaml. Without
    --target-config-dir, `config`/`experiments` are absent and this degrades to
    an empty cell rather than failing the render. -#}
{%- macro exp_links(experiment, include_logs=true) -%}
{%- set ns = namespace(out="", sep="") -%}
{%- if include_logs and config and config.report and config.report.per_experiment_logs -%}
{%- set ns.out = "[logs](" ~ (config.report.per_experiment_logs | interpolate(experiment=experiment)) ~ ")" -%}
{%- set ns.sep = " " -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- if experiments and experiment in experiments -%}
{%- for l in experiments[experiment].report_links -%}
{%- set ns.out = ns.out ~ ns.sep ~ "[" ~ l.text ~ "](" ~ (l.link | interpolate(experiment=experiment)) ~ ")" -%}
{%- set ns.sep = " " -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- endif -%}
{{- ns.out -}}
{%- endmacro -%}
{#- ---------------------------------------------------------------- checks -#}
{%- set bounds = checks | selectattr("kind", "equalto", "bounds") | list -%}
{%- set gates = bounds | selectattr("is_required") | list -%}
{%- set gates_failed = gates | rejectattr("passed") | list -%}
{%- set gates_passed = gates | selectattr("passed") | list -%}
{#- Non-gate checks never fail CI: informational bounds on non-quality_gate_*
    experiments, plus quantile checks, which are not severity-classified. -#}
{%- set other_checks = (bounds | rejectattr("is_required") | list)
                     + (checks | selectattr("kind", "equalto", "quantile") | list) -%}
{%- macro check_table(rows) -%}
| experiment | check | replicates | observed | links |
|---|---|---|---|---|
{% for c in rows -%}
| {{ c.experiment }} | {{ c.check_name }} | {{ c.replicates_passed }} | {{ "✅" if c.passed else "❌" }} {{ c.observed_value }} | {{ exp_links(c.experiment, false) }} |
{% endfor -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{#- ---------------------------------------------------- optimization goals -#}
{#- Decorate each goal row with its label, dot, tag and sort key. The verdict
    itself comes from the report: `change_detected` already encodes the
    effect-size, confidence-interval and configured-erratic criteria, so there
    is no statistics to re-derive here. -#}
{%- set ns = namespace(rows=[]) -%}
{%- for g in optimization_goals -%}
{%- set info = GOALS[g.goal] or [g.goal, "down"] -%}
{%- set tag = " **(ignored)**" if g.ignores_regressions else (" **(erratic)**" if g.is_erratic else "") -%}
{#- `rank` is the negated goal-relative change, so ascending order puts the
    worst-for-this-goal first and a throughput drop sorts alongside a memory
    rise rather than at the opposite end of the table.

    `improved`: `is_regression` in report.json means "a significant change was
    detected" and `is_improvement` gives its direction (Perf::from_result in
    SMP's view.rs), so the view's `is_improvement` is already the conjunction
    and a significant improvement is `change_detected and is_improvement`. -#}
{%- set ns.rows = ns.rows + [dict(
      experiment = g.experiment,
      name = g.experiment ~ tag,
      label = info[0],
      delta = g.delta_mean_percent_display,
      ci = g.ci_display,
      regressed = g.is_ci_fail,
      improved = (g.change_detected and g.is_improvement),
      dot = ("🟢" if (g.change_detected and g.is_improvement) else ("🔴" if g.is_ci_fail else "⚪")),
      rank = ((0 - g.delta_mean_percent) if info[1] == "down" else g.delta_mean_percent),
    )] -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- set rows = ns.rows | sort(attribute="rank,experiment") -%}
{%- set regressions = rows | selectattr("regressed") | list -%}
{%- set others = rows | rejectattr("regressed") | list -%}
{%- set improvements = rows | selectattr("improved") | list -%}
{%- macro goal_table(rows) -%}
| experiment | goal | Δ mean % | Δ mean % CI | links |
|---|---|---|---|---|
{% for r in rows -%}
| {{ r.name }} | {{ r.label }} | {{ r.dot }} {{ r.delta }} | {{ r.ci }} | {{ exp_links(r.experiment) }} |
{% endfor -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{#- -------------------------------------------------------------- document

  Whitespace discipline, because minijinja trimming is easy to get wrong and a
  `</details>` glued to the next block makes GitHub swallow the following
  markdown into the HTML block: every optional block below is written as

      {%- if cond %}
      <blank line>
      content
      {%- endif %}

  so it contributes exactly "\n\n" + content and leaves no trailing newline.
  That guarantees a blank line between every pair of rendered blocks.
-#}
